<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sanatana Goswami</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1488–<a href="/years/1558">1558</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sanatana Goswami was a theologian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaudiya-vaishnavism">Gaudiya Vaishnavism</a>, one of the six Goswamis of Vrindavan and elder brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupa-goswami">Rupa Goswami</a>. He served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-husain-shah">Alauddin Husain Shah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> as a minister, was imprisoned when he tried to resign, and escaped to join Chaitanya. At Vrindavan he wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> works on Vaishnava ritual and theology, including the Brihad-bhagavatamrita, and helped restore its sacred sites.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The family traced its descent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and later settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, at Ramakeli in Gauda. Sanatana was the eldest of three brothers, with Rupa and Vallabha, whom Chaitanya later named Anupama. His birthplace is disputed: Jessore, Naihati, Bakla Chandradvipa, Fateyabad and Ramakeli have all been proposed. The brothers studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyaya">Nyaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a> under the logician Vasudeva Sarvabhauma Bhattacharya and his brother Madhusudana Vidyavacaspati, from whom Sanatana took initiation as a child. On his father's death he had to take the post of Sakara Mallik, treasurer, under the new ruler of Bengal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1514, at Ramakeli, the brothers met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaitanya-mahaprabhu">Chaitanya Mahaprabhu</a> as he passed through Gauda on his way to Vrindavan. Sanatana warned him that a pilgrimage with thousands of followers was unfitting, and Chaitanya turned back after going as far as Kanair Natshala. The brothers resolved to join him, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> refused Sanatana's resignation. Sanatana stayed away from court feigning illness until the Sultan's physicians found him well, and when he refused to go with the Sultan on a campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> he was imprisoned.</p><p>A letter from Rupa told him that Chaitanya had left Puri for Vrindavan. Sanatana bribed the jailer with money Rupa had sent for emergencies, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> and found Chaitanya at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>. There he was taught that each soul is by nature an eternal servant of God, and that the teaching falls under three heads: sambandha, the relationship with the Godhead; abhidheya, the means of reviving it; and prayojana, the final goal. Later, at Puri, Chaitanya charged him to write on devotion, recover the places of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>'s pastimes, establish deity worship in Vrindavan and set out rules of conduct for devotees.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>At Vrindavan Sanatana located lost holy places and established the worship of the deity Madana-mohana, for whom Krishna Dasa Kapura, a rich officer in the Moghul army, built what became the Radha Madan Mohan Temple, one of the seven principal temples of Vrindavan. His samadhi stands beside it.</p><p>Of his four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> books, the first has 2,500 verses in two parts, on the ranks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>'s devotees and on the soul's journey to Krishna's eternal realm, with his own commentary, the Dig-darshini. The Hari-bhakti-vilasa, written with Gopala Bhatta Goswami, deals with the rituals and conduct of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaudiya-vaishnavism">Gaudiya Vaishnavas</a>; the Krishna-lila-stava retells Krishna's pastimes in 432 verses; and the Brihad Vaishnava Toshani comments on the Tenth Canto of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavata-purana">Bhagavata Purana</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sanatana Goswami” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Husain Shah employed Hindus in high office, among them the brothers Rupa and <b>Sanatana</b>, who later became leading theologians of the Gaudiya Vaishnava movement of Chaitanya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 16th century Vallabha preached a path of grace, and Chaitanya’s followers at Vrindavan, above all Rupa and <b>Sanatana Goswami</b>, set out a theology that analysed the emotions of love for Krishna.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
